Alright, picture a movie poster where someone confused “blockbuster” with “block of cheddar” and decided the only way to market an AI is to slap an elephant on it.

There’s a colossal elephant with eyes glowing like bad Wi‑Fi signals, looming over a city like it’s the final boss in a pachyderm-themed video game. Its trunk looks less like a trunk and more like a data pipeline that’s been overclocked and is now spraying fire and paperwork everywhere.

Below it, tiny human silhouettes are running, jumping, and doing what every action movie extra does best — dramatically flailing while cars and helicopters try to explain insurance claims. The title “LLM CHAIN” sits bold and smug at the bottom, as if to say, “Yes, we merged large language models with large mammal problems.”

Punchline: It’s basically “Jurassic Park” meets “tech conference” — the elephant never forgets your prompts, and apparently it holds grudges.
